# Where the tarball beside this file came from (#98). Update all three lines together on a bump,
# then re-run `make seed`.
#
# THIS ONE IS BUILT, NOT DOWNLOADED — it is our own app (ADR-0003), so there is no upstream release
# to fetch. `url=` names the tag it was built from rather than a download link, and the build is one
# command, run from the repo root:
#
#   git -C apps/epidemiologia archive --format=tar --prefix=epidemiologia/ v0.8.0 \
#     | gzip -n9 > provisioning/apps/epidemiologia/epidemiologia-0.8.0.tar.gz
#
# `--prefix=epidemiologia/` because ensure_vendored_app unpacks into custom_apps/ and Nextcloud
# needs the app id as the top directory — the same layout every other tarball here has. `gzip -n`
# keeps the mtime out of the header, so rebuilding the same tag yields the same bytes and the
# sha256 below stays checkable.
#
# TAG AFTER THE REBUILD, not before. @nextcloud/vite-config reads appinfo/info.xml and bakes
# <version> into the bundle, so a release commit that raises the version without running
# `npm run build` leaves js/ saying the OLD number inside an app that says the new one. v0.4.0 was
# cut that way and had to be moved. `scripts/smoke.sh` is what catches it: it builds, then compares
# the tree. Run it before tagging.
#
# `git archive` exports the TRACKED tree, which is why 288 MB of node_modules and 14 MB of tools/
# are not in here: both are gitignored in the app's repo. Result is 576 KB over 113 files.
#
# ONE tarball in this directory, ever: ensure_vendored_app globs *.tar.gz and fails on anything
# other than exactly one. A bump deletes the previous file, it does not sit beside it.
